# Hoare Programs

This page carries the checked-program claim-site at the computing surface.

* claim-site: a program as precondition, move, postcondition, and preservation;
* phase: computing after articulation has produced moves that can be checked;
* generating sites: traces, Tarski settling, and keratin lock histories;
* downstream uses: runtime verification pages, material-state programs, and
  shroud-indexed keratin pages;
* Lean carrier: {name Computing.HoareProgram}`Computing.HoareProgram` and
  {name Computing.KeratinousHoareProgram}`Computing.KeratinousHoareProgram`;
* Blueprint nodes: `def:hoare-program`, `thm:keratinous-hoare-program`, and
  `thm:keratinous-hoare-least-compatible`;
* provenance: the keratin contact-lock chapter and the causal-carrier note on
  keratinous Hoare morphisms;
* familiar comparison: the ordinary Hoare triple `{P} c {Q}`;
* reader closure: read `{P} c {Q}` as a carried preservation proof, not as an
  external annotation on untyped code;
* frontier status: there is not yet a parser from a source language into these
  carriers.

The governing sentence is:

> A Hoare program is a move whose preservation proof is part of the carrier.

## The Checked Program Carrier

The ordinary carrier is:

{docstring Computing.HoareProgram}

Its preservation proof is exposed directly:

{docstring Computing.HoareProgram.preserves_post}

Checked:

```lean
example {Input Output : Type}
    (P : Computing.HoareProgram Input Output)
    (input : Input) (hpre : P.precondition input) :
    P.postcondition (P.move input) :=
  P.preserves_post input hpre
```

This is the strict reading of "Hoare program" used here. The program is not a
bare command decorated by a later assertion. It is the move together with the
proof that the specified precondition is carried to the specified postcondition.

## The Keratinous Move

The keratinous specialization is:

{docstring Computing.KeratinousHoareProgram}

The move is not arbitrary. It first applies vertexwise irreversible lock
histories to a keratin seed and then settles the locked seed:

{docstring Computing.KeratinousHoareProgram.move}

Forgetting the material origin gives an ordinary Hoare program:

{docstring Computing.KeratinousHoareProgram.toHoareProgram}

Checked:

```lean
example {S : Order.CompleteLattice}
    (P : Computing.KeratinousHoareProgram S) :
    ∀ seed,
      P.precondition seed →
        P.postcondition (P.toHoareProgram.move seed) :=
  Computing.keratinousHoareProgram_derives_hoare_program P
```

This is the narrow meaning of "keratinous Hoare program": a Hoare program whose
move is generated by material locking and settling, not merely a Hoare triple
that happens to mention keratin.

## Least Compatible Post-State

The same carrier keeps the material law inherited from keratin settling:

{docstring Computing.keratinousHoareProgram_least_compatible_state}

Checked:

```lean
example {S : Order.CompleteLattice}
    (P : Computing.KeratinousHoareProgram S)
    (seed x : Tarski.Assign P.complex)
    (hcomp : Tarski.IsCompatible P.complex x)
    (hx : ∀ v, S.le (P.lockedSeed seed v) (x v)) :
    (Tarski.AssignCL S P.complex.Vertex).le
      (P.toHoareProgram.move seed) x :=
  Computing.KeratinousHoareProgram.move_least_compatible
    P seed x hcomp hx
```

Thus the post-state is not only postcondition-preserving. Relative to the locked
seed, it is the least compatible state. That is the material thickening: the
program move carries a Hoare proof and a keratin least-state law at the same
site.

## Frontier Status

The Lean surface now supplies the carrier and its keratinous specialization. It
does not yet supply a source-language semantics, weakest-precondition calculus,
or executable extraction from RMN terms into Hoare-program carriers.
